.TH "daharness\-kernel" "1" "" ""
.SH NAME
daharness\-kernel \(em Jupyter tool kernel for the daharness framework
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]daharness\-kernel\f[R] [\f[I]\(enkernel\f[R] |
\f[I]\(eninstall\-kernelspec\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Launches an ipython kernel (\f[I]tool_repl.py \(enkernel\f[R]) whose
namespace is the daharness tool surface, attachable from any local
Jupyter frontend.
It runs under the \f[I]daharness\-kernel.service\f[R] systemd unit in
the system profile.
.PP
Framework tools resolve in the kernel as \f[B]bare tool names\f[R]: no
imports, and no collision risk.
Every call must be \f[B]awaited\f[R] \(em the namespace injects async
wrappers that dispatch through \f[CR]run_tool\f[R]; without
\f[I]await\f[R] you receive a coroutine object and the tool never
executes.
.PP
Results arrive wrapped in a transport envelope:
.IP
.EX
{status, result, _elapsed_s}
.EE
.PP
The tool\(cqs own fields sit under \f[I]result\f[R].
Envelope helpers ship in the namespace (auto\-unwrapping):
\f[B]keys\f[R](value), \f[B]pluck\f[R](value, key),
\f[B]find\f[R](value, key), \f[B]grep\f[R](value, pattern).
Prefer these over guessing the nesting.
.SH INSTALLING THE KERNELSPEC
Frontends find the kernel through its kernelspec.
Register it once:
.IP
.EX
daharness\-kernel \-\-install\-kernelspec
.EE
.PP
This writes \f[I]daharness/kernel.json\f[R] into the invoking user\(cqs
kernel directory (\f[I]\(ti/.local/share/jupyter/kernels\f[R]); the spec
points back at the packaged \f[I]tool_repl.py \(enkernel\f[R], so
\f[CR]jupyter kernelspec list\f[R] showing \f[I]daharness\f[R] is the
attach\-ready signal and
\f[CR]jupyter kernelspec uninstall daharness\f[R] removes it.
Under sudo the installer resolves the real owner via
\f[B]DAHARNESS_KERNEL_OWNER\f[R] (fallback: SUDO_UID, else file owner)
and lands the spec in that user\(cqs directory, not in \f[I]/root\f[R].
\f[I]daharness\-repl \(eninstall\-kernelspec\f[R] is the same
passthrough under a different wrapper.
The kernel dispatches through the same Brain socket as the services lane
(see FILES).
.SH DEPENDENCIES
The kernel lane hard\-imports the Jupyter closure, so the deb ships it
as Depends: \f[B]python3\-ipykernel\f[R] (pulls
\f[I]python3\-ipython\f[R], \f[I]python3\-jupyter\-client\f[R],
\f[I]python3\-zmq\f[R], \f[I]python3\-tornado\f[R]); Kali builds these
against the system python.
In a checkout, restore the lane with
\f[CR]pip install \-e \(dq.[jupyter]\(dq\f[R].
Without it the unit fails fast \(em kernel_lane prints the remediation
string above the traceback (journalctl \-u daharness\-kernel).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]DAHARNESS_KERNEL_OWNER\f[R]
Name or uid to chown the kernel connection file and runtime artifacts
to, so the attaching operator\(cqs IDE can reach them.
Empty falls back to the uid inferred by the launcher (SUDO_UID, else
file owner).
.SH FILES
\f[I]/run/daharness/brain.sock\f[R] \(em shared with
\f[I]daharness(1)\f[R]; the kernel dispatches tools through the same
Brain socket.
